### Summary

Every `Miniflare#dispatchFetch()` (and `getWorker().fetch()`) closes its TCP connection after the response: `DispatchFetchDispatcher.dispatch` unconditionally sets undici's `options.reset = true` ([packages/miniflare/src/http/fetch.ts, "avoid keep-alive race conditions"](https://github.com/cloudflare/workers-sdk/blob/main/packages/miniflare/src/http/fetch.ts)). There is no opt-out: `dispatchFetch` builds the dispatcher internally and overwrites any `dispatcher` passed in the request init.

On macOS each closed connection then sits in TIME_WAIT for 2×MSL (30s by default), holding one ephemeral port from the machine-wide pool of 16,384 (`net.inet.ip.portrange` 49152–65535). Measured: 200 sequential `dispatchFetch` calls produce exactly 200 TIME_WAIT sockets; a miniflare-heavy integration suite of ours produces ~7,400 in 26 seconds. With a few such suites running concurrently on one CI host, the pool empties and every loopback `connect()`/`listen(0)` on the machine — including workerd's own socket binds and unrelated jobs — fails with `EADDRNOTAVAIL` for tens of seconds. We root-caused a recurring "fetch failed … EADDRNOTAVAIL" CI flake to exactly this.

### Versions

- `miniflare@5.20260811.1-alpha` (also present on `main` today — the `options.reset = true` line in `packages/miniflare/src/http/fetch.ts`)
- Node v22.23.2, macOS arm64 (Linux has a larger default ephemeral range and shorter TIME_WAIT recycling, so macOS hits this first)

### Minimal reproduction

```js
import { execSync } from "node:child_process";
import { Miniflare } from "miniflare";

const tw = () =>
  Number(execSync("netstat -an -p tcp | grep -c TIME_WAIT || true", { encoding: "utf8" }).trim());

const mf = new Miniflare({
  compatibilityDate: "2026-08-08",
  modules: true,
  script: `export default { async fetch() { return new Response("ok"); } };`,
});
await mf.ready;
const before = tw();
for (let i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
  const response = await mf.dispatchFetch("https://example.com/");
  await response.text();
}
console.log("TIME_WAIT delta:", tw() - before); // 200 — one connection per request
await mf.dispose();
```

### Ask

The comment above the flag says it mitigates keep-alive connections "disconnecting unexpectedly", so there may be a real race being avoided — but the mitigation costs one ephemeral port per request, machine-wide, with no escape hatch. Either of these would resolve it:

1. Reuse connections for runtime dispatches (undici already retries idempotent requests on a stale keep-alive connection, which may cover the original race), or
2. An option to opt out of `reset: true` (e.g. honoring a caller-supplied `dispatcher`, or a `Miniflare` constructor option), so CI-heavy users can choose connection reuse.

Happy to provide more measurements if useful.

# Loss-time selection can discard an earlier deadline

## Summary
quiche's `loss_time_and_space()` directly compares `Option<Instant>` in both legacy recovery and gcongestion recovery. In Rust, `None < Some(deadline)` is `true`, so a later empty `loss_time` can overwrite an existing valid deadline. As a result, time-threshold loss detection can be skipped and the implementation can fall through to the PTO or timer-clear path.

## Standard Requirement

[RFC 9002 Section 6.2.1, Loss Detection Timer](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9002.html#section-6.2.1):

```text
GetLossTimeAndSpace():
  time = loss_time[Initial]
  space = Initial
  for pn_space in [ Handshake, ApplicationData ]:
    if (time == 0 || loss_time[pn_space] < time):
      time = loss_time[pn_space];
      space = pn_space
  return time, space
```

The same section then requires:

```text
SetLossDetectionTimer():
  earliest_loss_time, _ = GetLossTimeAndSpace()
  if (earliest_loss_time != 0):
    // Time threshold loss detection.
    loss_detection_timer.update(earliest_loss_time)
    return
```

```text
OnLossDetectionTimeout():
  earliest_loss_time, pn_space = GetLossTimeAndSpace()
  if (earliest_loss_time != 0):
    // Time threshold loss Detection
    lost_packets = DetectAndRemoveLostPackets(pn_space)
```

The standard semantics are: when any non-zero `loss_time` exists, select the earliest deadline and its packet number space, then prioritize time-threshold loss detection.

## Relevant Source Code

`quiche/src/recovery/congestion/recovery.rs:440`

```rust
fn loss_time_and_space(&self) -> (Option<Instant>, Epoch) {
    let mut epoch = Epoch::Initial;
    let mut time = self.epochs[epoch].loss_time;

    for e in [Epoch::Handshake, Epoch::Application] {
        let new_time = self.epochs[e].loss_time;
        if time.is_none() || new_time < time {
            time = new_time;
            epoch = e;
        }
    }

    (time, epoch)
}
```

`quiche/src/recovery/gcongestion/recovery.rs:597`

```rust
fn loss_time_and_space(&self) -> (Option<Instant>, packet::Epoch) {
    let mut epoch = packet::Epoch::Initial;
    let mut time = self.epochs[epoch].loss_time;

    for e in [packet::Epoch::Handshake, packet::Epoch::Application] {
        let new_time = self.epochs[e].loss_time;
        if time.is_none() || new_time < time {
            time = new_time;
            epoch = e;
        }
    }

    (time, epoch)
}
```

The callers trust this helper:

`quiche/src/recovery/congestion/recovery.rs:507`

```rust
let (earliest_loss_time, _) = self.loss_time_and_space();

if let Some(to) = earliest_loss_time {
    self.loss_timer.update(to);
    return;
}
```

`quiche/src/recovery/congestion/recovery.rs:764`

```rust
let (earliest_loss_time, epoch) = self.loss_time_and_space();

if earliest_loss_time.is_some() {
    let (lost_packets, lost_bytes) =
        self.detect_lost_packets(epoch, now, trace_id);
```

## Implementation Behavior

When `Initial = Some(30)`, `Handshake = Some(10)`, and `Application = None`, the correct result is `Some(10), Handshake`. The current condition `new_time < time` treats `None` as smaller than `Some(10)`, so the final result is `None, Application`.

That means `set_loss_detection_timer()` cannot see the existing deadline, and `on_loss_detection_timeout()` does not enter the time-threshold loss-detection branch.

## Inconsistency Reason

The standard requires selecting the earliest non-zero loss deadline and using it to drive the loss detection timer. quiche treats "no deadline" (`None`) as a value that can defeat an existing deadline, so the returned value no longer represents the earliest loss time. The evidence supports the described behavior.

Note: the [RFC 9002 Section 6.2.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9002.html#section-6.2.1) pseudocode is not defensive about `0` candidates, but in timer semantics `0` means there is no loss time and must not overwrite an existing non-zero deadline.

## Runtime Evidence

A minimal Rust probe was run:

```text
rustc --edition=2021 runtime/loss_time_option_probe.rs -o runtime/loss_time_option_probe.exe
runtime/loss_time_option_probe.exe
```

The probe compared the relevant `Option<Instant>` ordering case and observed:

```text
case=[Some(30), Some(10), None]
actual=(None, Application)
expected=(Some(10), Handshake)
none_lt_some=true
```

The auxiliary source probe was also re-run and reported `loss_time_option_selection_bug` as `ok: true`, confirming that the source still contains the same direct `Option<Instant>` comparison in both recovery implementations.

## Impact

If an earlier packet number space has a time-threshold loss deadline while a later space has no deadline, quiche can clear or skip that loss deadline and delay or miss loss detection that should run immediately.

## Fix Direction

Ignore `None` candidates during comparison. Allow only `Some(candidate)` to replace the current value when the current value is empty or the candidate is earlier:

```rust
if let Some(new_time) = new_time {
    if time.is_none() || Some(new_time) < time {
        time = Some(new_time);
        epoch = e;
    }
}
```

# 0-RTT rejection keeps recovery state

## Problem Description

[RFC 9002 Section 6.4](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9002.html#section-6.4) requires discarding recovery state for all in-flight 0-RTT packets when 0-RTT is rejected. quiche detects TLS early-data rejection, but the client-side recovery state for the sent 0-RTT packet remains in the Application packet number space.

## Standard Requirement

[RFC 9002 Section 6.4, Discarding Keys and Packet State](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9002.html#section-6.4):

```text
When 0-RTT is rejected, recovery state for all in-flight 0-RTT
packets is discarded.
```

The same section also says packets whose keys are discarded must be removed from bytes in flight.

## Relevant Source Code

`implementions/quiche/quiche/src/tls/mod.rs:644-647`

```rust
// SSL_ERROR_EARLY_DATA_REJECTED
15 => {
    self.reset_early_data_reject();
    Err(Error::Done)
},
```

TLS reports early-data rejection, resets BoringSSL's rejection state, and returns `Done`; no QUIC recovery cleanup is triggered here.

`implementions/quiche/quiche/src/lib.rs:8847-8858`

```rust
fn drop_epoch_state(&mut self, epoch: packet::Epoch, now: Instant) {
    let crypto_ctx = &mut self.crypto_ctx[epoch];
    if crypto_ctx.crypto_open.is_none() {
        return;
    }
    crypto_ctx.clear();
    self.pkt_num_spaces[epoch].clear();

    let handshake_status = self.handshake_status();
    for (_, p) in self.paths.iter_mut() {
        p.recovery
            .on_pkt_num_space_discarded(epoch, handshake_status, now);
```

quiche has a generic path that can drop packet-number-space state and call recovery cleanup, but the early-data rejection path does not call it for 0-RTT/Application state.

`implementions/quiche/quiche/src/recovery/congestion/recovery.rs:843-866`

```rust
fn on_pkt_num_space_discarded(
    &mut self, epoch: Epoch, handshake_status: HandshakeStatus, now: Instant,
) {
    let epoch = &mut self.epochs[epoch];

    let unacked_bytes = epoch
        .sent_packets
        .iter()
        .filter(|p| {
            p.in_flight && p.time_acked.is_none() && p.time_lost.is_none()
        })
        .fold(0, |acc, p| acc + p.size);

    self.bytes_in_flight.saturating_subtract(unacked_bytes, now);

    epoch.sent_packets.clear();
    epoch.clear_lost_frames();
    epoch.acked_frames.clear();
```

Legacy recovery would remove sent packet state and subtract in-flight bytes if invoked.

`implementions/quiche/quiche/src/recovery/gcongestion/recovery.rs:1008-1015`

```rust
fn on_pkt_num_space_discarded(
    &mut self, epoch: packet::Epoch, handshake_status: HandshakeStatus,
    now: Instant,
) {
    let epoch = &mut self.epochs[epoch];
    self.bytes_in_flight
        .saturating_subtract(epoch.discard(&mut self.pacer), now);
```

gcongestion has equivalent cleanup for discarded packet-number-space state.

## Runtime Evidence

Temporary test added to `implementions/quiche/quiche/src/tests.rs` and removed after execution. It creates a resumable session with 0-RTT enabled, then starts a new connection where the client sends 0-RTT but the server configuration rejects early data. After the client processes the server flight and leaves early-data state, the test expects Application recovery state to be empty.

```rust
assert!(pipe.client.is_in_early_data());
assert_eq!(pipe.client.stream_send(4, b"hello", true), Ok(5));
let (len, _) = pipe.client.send(&mut buf).unwrap();
let _zrtt = buf[..len].to_vec();

let recovery = &pipe.client.paths.get_active().unwrap().recovery;
assert!(recovery.sent_packets_len(packet::Epoch::Application) > 0);
assert!(recovery.bytes_in_flight() > 0);

assert_eq!(pipe.server_recv(&mut initial), Ok(initial.len()));
let flight = test_utils::emit_flight(&mut pipe.server).unwrap();
test_utils::process_flight(&mut pipe.client, flight).unwrap();
assert!(!pipe.client.is_in_early_data());

let recovery = &pipe.client.paths.get_active().unwrap().recovery;
assert_eq!(
    (
        recovery.sent_packets_len(packet::Epoch::Application),
        recovery.bytes_in_flight()
    ),
    (0, 0)
);
```

Command:

```text
cd implementions/quiche/quiche
cargo test --release zero_rtt_rejection_discards_recovery_state -- --nocapture
```

Result:

```text
running 2 tests
cc_algorithm_name_1___cubic__ ... FAILED
cc_algorithm_name_2___bbr2_gcongestion__ ... FAILED

assertion `left == right` failed
left: (1, 68)
right: (0, 0)

assertion `left == right` failed
left: (1, 68)
right: (0, 0)
```

Both recovery backends retain one Application-space sent packet and 68 bytes in flight after 0-RTT is rejected.

## Inconsistency Reason

The standard requires in-flight 0-RTT recovery state to be discarded and removed from bytes in flight. quiche exits early-data state after rejection, but recovery still reports `(sent_packets_len, bytes_in_flight) = (1, 68)` for the Application packet number space.

## Impact

Rejected 0-RTT data can remain visible to loss recovery and congestion accounting. That can keep stale bytes in flight and allow later PTO/loss processing to reason about packets whose 0-RTT keys/data should already have been discarded.

## Fix Direction

When TLS reports 0-RTT rejection to the client, discard recovery state for in-flight 0-RTT packets and subtract their bytes from bytes in flight. Because quiche stores 0-RTT in the Application packet number space, the fix should avoid discarding valid 1-RTT state if any can coexist in that space.

## Summary

`cloudflare_email_routing_settings` accepts `support_subaddress` in configuration as of v5.26.0, but the value is never sent to the API. `Create()` calls `EmailRouting.Enable` with `ZoneID` only, and `Update()` is an empty function. The result is that the attribute cannot be set, and any configuration that sets it produces a plan diff that never converges.

This is the write half of #7301. That issue was about the schema/model mismatch crashing state refresh; the fix in 5.26.0 (`email_routing_settings: add missing support_subaddress to resource schema`) added the attribute to the schema, which unblocked refresh but left the attribute non-functional. I am filing separately rather than reopening #7301 because the crash there really is fixed.

## Versions

- `terraform-provider-cloudflare`: v5.26.0
- Terraform CLI: 1.16.2

## Reproducer

```hcl
resource "cloudflare_email_routing_settings" "zone" {
  zone_id            = var.zone_id
  support_subaddress = true
}
```

## Actual behavior

`terraform apply` reports success, but subaddressing stays disabled. `GET /zones/{zone_id}/email/routing` keeps returning `"support_subaddress": false`, and the next `terraform plan` shows the same diff again:

```
  ~ resource "cloudflare_email_routing_settings" "zone" {
      ~ support_subaddress = false -> true
    }
```

Every subsequent apply repeats this, so a configuration containing this attribute can never reach an empty plan.

## Expected behavior

`support_subaddress = true` enables subaddressing, and the following plan is empty.

## Root cause (observed in v5.26.0 source)

`internal/services/email_routing_settings/resource.go`:

```go
func (r *EmailRoutingSettingsResource) Create(ctx context.Context, req resource.CreateRequest, resp *resource.CreateResponse) {
	// ...
	_, err := r.client.EmailRouting.Enable(
		ctx,
		email_routing.EmailRoutingEnableParams{
			ZoneID: cloudflare.F(data.ZoneID.ValueString()),   // no SupportSubaddress
		},
		// ...
	)
```

```go
func (r *EmailRoutingSettingsResource) Update(ctx context.Context, req resource.UpdateRequest, resp *resource.UpdateResponse) {
	// Update is not supported for this resource
}
```

`Read()` then overwrites state from `GET /zones/{zone_id}/email/routing`, which is what turns the discarded value into a perpetual diff rather than a silent no-op.

Note also that `POST /zones/{zone_id}/email/routing/enable` — the endpoint `Enable` targets — is marked **Deprecated** in the API docs, while `PATCH /zones/{zone_id}/email/routing` ("Update Email Routing settings") is documented as accepting `enabled`, `skip_wizard` and `support_subaddress`.

## The SDK already has what is needed

The provider imports `github.com/cloudflare/cloudflare-go/v7` (v7.11.0 in `go.mod`), and that package already exposes the parameters:

```go
// email_routing/emailrouting.go
type EmailRoutingEditParams struct {
	// ...
	SupportSubaddress param.Field[EmailRoutingEditParamsSupportSubaddress] `json:"support_subaddress"`
}
```

`EmailRoutingUpdateParams` (PUT) carries the same field. So the fix looks like wiring `Create`/`Update` to `Edit` (or `Update`) instead of the deprecated `Enable`, rather than any SDK or API work.

## Why this matters / no workaround inside Terraform

`support_subaddress` is the switch that decides whether `user+tag@example.com` is matched against the `user@example.com` routing rule. With it off, plus-addressed mail matches no rule and the sender gets a bounce, so for anyone routing plus-addressed mail to an Email Worker it is a required setting, not a nicety.

There is no other resource that can set it: `cloudflare_email_routing_dns` exposes `support_subaddress` as `Computed` only. The only options today are toggling it in the dashboard (leaving it unmanaged) or calling the API from a provisioner. For what it is worth, a `check` block is a reasonable stopgap for detecting the unmanaged setting being off:

```hcl
check "subaddressing" {
  assert {
    condition     = cloudflare_email_routing_dns.this.support_subaddress
    error_message = "Subaddressing is disabled; plus-addressed mail will bounce."
  }
}
```

but that only reports the problem — it cannot fix it.

