### Description

On non-Node runtimes (React Native / Hermes), every `streamText` run that ends without a finished step leaves a rejected promise with no handler. That includes an aborted run (a user Stop), a stall timeout, and an error. React Native reports each one as `Uncaught (in promise, id: 0) AbortError: This operation was aborted`, which shows as a LogBox error in dev builds.

`streamText` passes its telemetry helper a promise derived from the run's total usage (`self._totalUsage.promise.then(() => void 0)`). When the run is aborted or fails, the stream's flush rejects the result promises (`rejectResultPromises`), so that derived promise rejects too.

In `packages/ai/src/telemetry/tracing-channel-publisher.ts`, `openTelemetryChannelSpanContext` handles `completion` when Node has no subscribers:

```ts
if (tracingChannel == null || tracingChannel.hasSubscribers === false || asyncHooks == null) {
  Promise.resolve(completion).catch(() => {});
  return undefined;
}
```

The early return for non-Node runtimes has no such handler:

```ts
if (!isNodeRuntime()) {
  return undefined;
}
```

So off Node, `completion` is never observed.

### Reproduction

Expo SDK 57, React Native 0.86, Hermes, `ai` 7.0.114 (also present in 7.0.116 and on `main`). Use `useChat` with a `DirectChatTransport` over a `ToolLoopAgent`, start a reply, and call `stop()` mid-stream. Every stop logs the unhandled rejection. In Jest, making `isNodeRuntime()` report false and aborting a `streamText` run reproduces it as an unhandled rejection that fails the test.

### Suggested fix

Mirror the no-subscriber branch:

```ts
if (!isNodeRuntime()) {
  Promise.resolve(completion).catch(() => {});
  return undefined;
}
```

We apply exactly this with patch-package, and the rejection is gone on device and in tests.

